1
00:00:00,670 --> 00:00:05,175
At 9:07 on Monday morning, Daniel Hale did
his manager a favour.

2
00:00:05,745 --> 00:00:09,287
By Thursday, a colleague was being blamed
for a mistake she hadn't made.

3
00:00:09,857 --> 00:00:13,827
Daniel knew the truth. So did one other
person: his manager.

4
00:00:14,267 --> 00:00:16,802
And his manager never once raised his
voice.

5
00:00:22,722 --> 00:00:23,918
Rewind to Monday.

6
00:00:24,098 --> 00:00:28,249
Daniel Hale, thirty-two. Reporting analyst
at a logistics firm.

7
00:00:28,429 --> 00:00:31,655
The person everyone sends their
spreadsheets to when they need them right.

8
00:00:33,415 --> 00:00:37,807
Leah: Final depot extract's in the folder.
Every depot, every delivery.

9
00:00:38,107 --> 00:00:41,602
Daniel: You're a star. I'll have the
client pack out by ten.

10
00:00:42,172 --> 00:00:45,630
Leah Carter joined eight months ago. She
builds the data.

11
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:48,412
Daniel turns it into the report the client
sees.

12
00:00:51,532 --> 00:00:53,860
Graham: Flat white, no sugar. Big day.

13
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:55,242
Daniel: You remembered.

14
00:00:55,712 --> 00:00:57,140
Graham: I always remember.

15
00:00:57,970 --> 00:01:01,685
He's patient, generous, and very good at
remembering things.

16
00:01:01,865 --> 00:01:05,395
Last month, he approved Daniel's leave at
a day's notice.

17
00:01:05,575 --> 00:01:08,951
Two weeks ago, he put Daniel's name
forward for promotion.

18
00:01:10,741 --> 00:01:13,049
Daniel likes him. Most people do.

19
00:01:14,639 --> 00:01:17,726
At 9:03, Daniel notices something small.

20
00:01:17,906 --> 00:01:21,535
A filter left over from a system migration
has removed one depot.

21
00:01:21,715 --> 00:01:29,317
With Northfield included, on-time delivery
is 92.6 percent. Without it, 93.4.

22
00:01:29,667 --> 00:01:32,268
The client's contract target is 93.

23
00:01:34,678 --> 00:01:36,756
Daniel: Northfield's been filtered out.

24
00:01:39,646 --> 00:01:43,025
Graham, got a minute? There's a filter on
the Ostrel dashboard.

25
00:01:43,325 --> 00:01:46,320
Northfield's been excluded since the
migration.

26
00:01:47,150 --> 00:01:50,784
Graham: Ah. That'll be mine. I set it up
for the cut-over.

27
00:01:51,254 --> 00:01:54,356
Daniel: Without it, we're under target.
Point eight under.

28
00:01:55,606 --> 00:02:00,908
Graham: The pack goes at ten. Flag it now,
and it's a week of calls over a rounding error.

29
00:02:01,548 --> 00:02:06,634
Leave it out of the notes for now.
I'll fix it at source before the quarter closes.

30
00:02:08,914 --> 00:02:10,621
Daniel: So we just... don't mention it?

31
00:02:11,701 --> 00:02:15,416
Graham: We correct it properly. Quietly.
No drama.

32
00:02:16,836 --> 00:02:18,362
I knew I could trust you.

33
00:02:23,522 --> 00:02:26,377
At 9:58, the report went out.

34
00:02:26,977 --> 00:02:29,600
Notes and exceptions: none this quarter.

35
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:31,552
It didn't feel like a lie.

36
00:02:31,992 --> 00:02:35,051
It felt like a favour, for someone who'd
done him plenty.

37
00:02:36,811 --> 00:02:39,655
On Tuesday afternoon, the client noticed.

38
00:02:42,215 --> 00:02:46,628
Ruth: Ostrel say our Q3 numbers don't
reconcile. Who built the data?

39
00:02:46,928 --> 00:02:50,841
Graham: The extract is Leah's. She's still
learning the depot feeds.

40
00:02:51,401 --> 00:02:55,666
Let's not pile on her. I'll take her
through the checks myself.

41
00:02:56,496 --> 00:02:58,533
Nothing Graham said was false.

42
00:02:58,913 --> 00:03:01,631
The extract was Leah's. She was still
learning.

43
00:03:02,711 --> 00:03:06,069
Daniel could have walked in. It would have
taken one sentence.

44
00:03:06,929 --> 00:03:08,445
He stayed at his desk.

45
00:03:13,715 --> 00:03:16,310
Leah: Ruth wants to go through my extract.

46
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:20,150
I've checked it three times. It's right,
isn't it?

47
00:03:20,870 --> 00:03:22,252
Daniel: It... looks right to me.

48
00:03:22,552 --> 00:03:24,444
Leah: Then where's the difference coming
from?

49
00:03:25,254 --> 00:03:26,676
Daniel: I'll take another look.

50
00:03:28,096 --> 00:03:30,268
That was true too. Every word.

51
00:03:31,938 --> 00:03:36,134
Graham: How's Leah holding up? Keep an eye
on her for me, would you?

52
00:03:36,694 --> 00:03:39,355
You're the one person here I can count on.

53
00:03:41,315 --> 00:03:45,483
From today, Leah's extracts get a second
check before they go out.

54
00:03:46,123 --> 00:03:48,364
No blame. Just process.

55
00:03:50,014 --> 00:03:51,499
Nobody said a word.

56
00:03:51,679 --> 00:03:54,271
Leah assumed everyone thought it was her
fault.

57
00:03:54,451 --> 00:03:56,890
Daniel assumed someone else would say
something.

58
00:03:57,330 --> 00:03:59,493
Each silence made the next one easier.

59
00:04:00,573 --> 00:04:05,356
Leah: You built the client pack. You'd
have seen every filter on that dashboard.

60
00:04:06,076 --> 00:04:07,094
Did you know?

61
00:04:08,244 --> 00:04:09,730
Daniel: Leah... it's complicated.

62
00:04:10,370 --> 00:04:12,064
Leah: No. It's really not.

63
00:04:16,344 --> 00:04:19,121
Let's pause. Daniel isn't a bad person.

64
00:04:19,561 --> 00:04:21,593
So why does a decent person stay quiet?

65
00:04:22,503 --> 00:04:25,671
Psychologists would point to at least
three quiet forces.

66
00:04:28,321 --> 00:04:29,891
The first is reciprocity.

67
00:04:30,241 --> 00:04:33,708
Sociologist Alvin Gouldner described a
near-universal norm:

68
00:04:33,888 --> 00:04:36,716
when someone helps us, we feel we owe
them.

69
00:04:36,896 --> 00:04:40,876
When Graham finally asked, saying no felt
like refusing to pay a debt.

70
00:04:41,706 --> 00:04:47,169
In one classic 1971 study, people given a
free soft drink by a stranger

71
00:04:47,349 --> 00:04:51,422
later bought about twice as many of his
raffle tickets as people given nothing.

72
00:04:52,732 --> 00:04:57,645
The second is cognitive dissonance: the
discomfort of acting against your own values.

73
00:04:58,085 --> 00:05:01,157
We often ease it by changing the story,
not the behaviour.

74
00:05:01,507 --> 00:05:06,394
It's only point eight percent. Graham will
fix it. It's not my place.

75
00:05:06,994 --> 00:05:12,808
Recent large replications found weaker effects, so
treat it as a tendency, not a law.

76
00:05:13,638 --> 00:05:15,711
The third is pluralistic ignorance.

77
00:05:15,891 --> 00:05:20,136
Many people privately doubt something, but
each assumes the others are fine with it.

78
00:05:20,316 --> 00:05:21,615
So nobody speaks.

79
00:05:22,135 --> 00:05:26,460
In one Princeton study, students thought
they were more uneasy about campus drinking

80
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:30,557
than their peers were. Many of their peers
felt exactly the same.

81
00:05:31,127 --> 00:05:35,481
Once you've agreed to something small, the
next request feels smaller than it is.

82
00:05:35,831 --> 00:05:38,328
Psychologists call it the foot in the
door.

83
00:05:47,688 --> 00:05:49,916
The first favour was to stay quiet.

84
00:05:50,356 --> 00:05:54,271
This one was to say something untrue, to a
director, about a colleague.

85
00:05:55,941 --> 00:05:58,104
Daniel couldn't know what Graham intended.

86
00:05:58,794 --> 00:06:00,470
But he could control four things:

87
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:05,306
what he wrote down, what he confirmed,
what he asked, and what he corrected.

88
00:06:06,216 --> 00:06:07,834
First, he wrote it down.

89
00:06:08,524 --> 00:06:11,356
Dates, times, facts. No adjectives.

90
00:06:14,116 --> 00:06:15,911
Then he confirmed it in writing.

91
00:06:17,621 --> 00:06:20,802
He asked a neutral question. No
accusation.

92
00:06:21,242 --> 00:06:23,474
Just the facts, and a way forward.

93
00:06:26,034 --> 00:06:29,361
Graham: Daniel. Did we really need all
that in an email?

94
00:06:29,921 --> 00:06:31,673
Daniel: I just want us to be accurate.

95
00:06:32,313 --> 00:06:34,454
Graham: I thought we were on the same
page.

96
00:06:35,264 --> 00:06:37,516
Daniel: We are. That's why I wrote it
down.

97
00:06:41,766 --> 00:06:46,151
Ruth: Before Ostrel join us: Daniel, Graham
says you can explain the data issue.

98
00:06:50,541 --> 00:06:52,202
Daniel: Leah's extract was correct.

99
00:06:52,762 --> 00:06:56,649
The difference is a filter on our
dashboard that excluded Northfield.

100
00:06:57,209 --> 00:07:01,281
I spotted it on Monday, before the report
went out, and told Graham.

101
00:07:04,111 --> 00:07:05,884
We agreed not to flag it.

102
00:07:06,604 --> 00:07:08,933
I should have corrected that on Tuesday.

103
00:07:09,573 --> 00:07:11,013
I'm correcting it now.

104
00:07:12,573 --> 00:07:13,573
Ruth: Graham?

105
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:16,010
Graham: That's... a fair summary.

106
00:07:16,650 --> 00:07:20,485
I meant to fix it before it mattered. I
should have been clearer.

107
00:07:21,315 --> 00:07:25,400
Calm. Reasonable. Not a raised voice, even
now.

108
00:07:26,870 --> 00:07:29,148
Ruth: Then Ostrel get the corrected figure
today.

109
00:07:29,708 --> 00:07:32,761
From now on, every dashboard change gets
logged.

110
00:07:33,571 --> 00:07:35,464
Leah, I'm sorry. You were right.

111
00:07:36,544 --> 00:07:39,496
And Daniel? Next time, say it on Tuesday.

112
00:07:40,216 --> 00:07:41,629
Daniel: Next time, Tuesday.

113
00:07:43,559 --> 00:07:45,272
Leah: You could have said it on Tuesday.

114
00:07:45,992 --> 00:07:46,992
Daniel: I know.

115
00:07:47,577 --> 00:07:52,234
Leah: Graham told me on Tuesday you'd
raised concerns about my extract.

116
00:07:53,044 --> 00:07:54,333
Daniel: I never said that.

117
00:07:55,053 --> 00:07:56,411
Leah: I know. Now.

118
00:07:57,321 --> 00:08:00,027
Graham had asked Daniel to keep an eye on
Leah.

119
00:08:00,627 --> 00:08:03,020
And he'd told Leah that Daniel had doubts
about her.

120
00:08:03,710 --> 00:08:05,714
Each of them had been watching the other.

121
00:08:07,644 --> 00:08:09,108
Was Graham a manipulator?

122
00:08:09,628 --> 00:08:14,859
Or just a likeable man protecting himself,
one small, reasonable request at a time?

123
00:08:15,719 --> 00:08:18,880
Daniel never found out for certain. He
didn't need to.

124
00:08:20,130 --> 00:08:22,195
Because the trap was never the favour.

125
00:08:22,885 --> 00:08:25,404
The manager didn't need everyone to trust
him.

126
00:08:26,004 --> 00:08:28,618
He only needed everyone to distrust each
other.

127
00:08:30,978 --> 00:08:33,125
Most favours are just kindness.

128
00:08:33,645 --> 00:08:37,139
The warning sign is a favour that asks you
to hide something.

129
00:08:37,739 --> 00:08:40,443
If that happens: write down what was
asked.

130
00:08:40,623 --> 00:08:42,060
Confirm it in writing.

131
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:43,694
Ask neutral questions.

132
00:08:43,874 --> 00:08:47,823
And correct the record early. Silence gets
more expensive every day.

133
00:08:49,953 --> 00:08:53,862
Have you ever been asked for a small favour
at work that turned out not to be small?

134
00:08:54,552 --> 00:08:57,073
What did you do, or what do you wish you'd
done?
